I've been thinking for a while now about this model of space-bounded complexity called $\mathsf{CL}$ (short for ``catalytic logspace'', because most of the space used for computation is restored unchanged to its initial configuration afterwards, kinda like a catalyst in a chemical reaction). 
In this post, I've finally sat down and tried to give as quick and easy a sense as I can for what this model is and how you can do things with it. The impetus for writing this was twofold: 
1) My friends discovered a beautifully simple new CL algorithm for reachability in directed graphs.
2) I came up with a proof of $\mathsf{TC}^1 \subseteq \mathsf{CL}$ (the foundational result in catalytic computing) that I personally think is easier than the original proof.
